so one of the biggest dividing lines we  could call it between the American left  and right is about what role should  government have in our lives how big  should government be what are the things  that government should and shouldn't be  doing and the public policy mood  estimate is a measure of exactly that  and it is at the most liberal point in  the 68 year history of the measure and  it's really important to understand that  this measure doesn't address the  public's view on a lot of the issues  that have been big parts of the Donald  Trump administration so immigration is  not addressed by the public policy mood  report trade is not in any way addressed  by this it's specifically related to the  role and scope of government in society  and I have to tell you the results are  not actually very surprising because  when we look at individual issue polls  where people are asked should the  government provide a basic level of  health care to everybody regardless of  whether they can pay for it the country  is the most to the left that it has been  on that issue people by and large  thinking the answer is yes the  government should provide some basic  level of care to everybody even if they  can't pay for it and part of the reason  why is that whether you're symp  sympathies to that idea our ideological  or moral or economic we now realize that  hey we're a well rich enough country  that we can be making sure everybody has  a basic level of care and it's also good  economically because when people have  access to health care all sorts of other  negative externalities are mitigated  like work absenteeism and productivity  if that is what you care about or  criminality related to needing money to  pay for medical treatments medical  bankruptcies all of that stuff whatever  your sympathies are with regards to  health care more and more of the country  now recognizes everybody is better off  if the government does something that we  can very easily afford to do which is  make sure everybody has some basic level  of care on the issue of Taxation should  the government be using taxation to  reduce income inequality by taxing the  very very rich the country has moved to  the left on that it has been a  significant and  that he move to the left now I know that  there are still people who don't want  the rich to be taxed I know there are  still people who believe that it's not  good economically to tax the very very  rich to lift the bottom end and make  sure nobody gets left behind you can  find those people many of them voted for  Trump but in total the country has moved  to the left in terms of believing that  that is a role that government should be  playing this is why Republicans don't  want people to vote when you understand  and recognize that on the issues the  country is well to the left of our  elected officials you don't want people  to vote the ideas that people have come  around to and this is bigger than Trump  are more or less the ideas of Franklin D  Roosevelt second Bill of Rights  FDR proposed this during his 1944 State  of the Union address the canoe the  elements of it were employment a bill of  rights that contains employment meaning  the right to work food clothing and  leisure with enough income to support  those activities a farmer's right to a  fair income this was very important in  the u.s. at the time freedom from unfair  competition and monopolies Elizabeth  Warren is talking about this housing  medical care social security and  education this is from 1944 I would add  to the second Bill of Rights now  protecting the environment in the  context of the climate crisis that we're  facing but this is all about  government's role and on government's  role in total the country is as left as  it has ever been so if we fix our  election system we might actually have a  shot at making more of these things a  reality because the most liberal public  mood ever is not going to get  progressive politicians elected if 